<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Colonial Brazil</h1><p class="st-meta">1500 CE – 1815 CE · capital Salvador</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Portugal's vast South American colony, built on sugar, gold and the labour of millions of enslaved Africans, from which modern Brazil grew.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-alvares-cabral">Pedro Álvares Cabral</a> reached the coast of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/new-republic-brazil">Brazil</a> in April 1500 and claimed it for Portugal under the Treaty of Tordesillas. Early decades were dominated by the trade in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazilwood">brazilwood</a>. From 1534 the crown divided the coast into hereditary captaincies, and in 1549 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tome-de-sousa">Tomé de Sousa</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador">Salvador</a> as the seat of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-brazil">State of Brazil</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The colony was governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> through governors-general and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroys</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> ran missions among the indigenous peoples. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">Sugar plantations</a> in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/captaincy-of-pernambuco">Pernambuco</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahia">Bahia</a> relied on enslaved Africans, and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/new-republic-brazil">Brazil</a> became the largest destination of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a>. Bandeirantes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-paulo">São Paulo</a> pushed into the interior, and gold was found in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/captaincy-of-minas-gerais">Minas Gerais</a> in the 1690s, moving the centre of gravity south. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a> became the capital in 1763.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1808 the Portuguese court fled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon's</a> invasion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a>, opening <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil's</a> ports. In 1815 it was raised to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-brazil">Kingdom of Brazil</a> within a <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom-of-great-britain-and-ireland">United Kingdom</a> with Portugal, ending its colonial status.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/colonial-brazil.jpg" alt="Colonial Brazil" width="320" height="308"><figcaption>Image: Floppa Historico · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mapa_do_Brasil_em_1534.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1500 CE – 1815 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Salvador</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Tomé de Sousa, Mem de Sá</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Portuguese, Tupi (língua geral)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/brazil">Brazil</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Portuguese Brazil, Brasil Colônia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1658&amp;focus=colonial-brazil" aria-label="Colonial Brazil on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3624 2661 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Colonial Brazil lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3944 2917L3481 2525M3944 2917L4773 1044M3944 2917L4773 1045M3944 2917L3845 3279M3944 2917L3765 3317M3944 2917L4036 2733M3944 2917L3944 2917M3944 2917L3936 3045M3944 2917L3933 2984M3944 2917L3914 3185M3944 2917L3845 3279M3944 2917L3718 3265M3944 2917L3814 3166M3944 2917L3714 2993M3944 2917L3454 